<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Naval Defence Act 1889</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Naval Defence Act 1889 was a British law that formally adopted the two-power standard, under which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> was to be at least as strong as the next two largest navies combined, then those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. It provided about 21.5 million pounds for the construction of 70 new warships, including ten battleships, and began a sustained expansion of the fleet that lasted until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The act was passed under the government of Lord Salisbury and received royal assent on 31 May 1889. Parliament had at first opposed higher naval spending, but expert opinions laid before it in December 1888 and February 1889 were critical of the state of the navy, and the growth of the French and Russian fleets seemed to point to British weakness. Public support for expansion grew and pressed Parliament to act.</p><p>The two-power standard was not new: it had been used informally for some seventy years, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> had briefly met it in the 1850s. Britain already held naval superiority, and the act's formal adoption of the standard signalled an ambition to raise that lead still further.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The battleships were the centrepiece. Eight first-class ships were ordered, seven of the Royal Sovereign class and a half-sister, HMS Hood, together with two second-class battleships, HMS Centurion and HMS Barfleur. The Royal Sovereign class was larger and faster than any Russian or French battleship. Cruisers of the Edgar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, Astraea and Pearl classes were to protect British supply lines, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torpedo">torpedo</a> gunboats to guard the battle fleet.</p><p>The First Lord of the Admiralty, Lord George Hamilton, argued that so large a programme would deter other powers and so lower spending later. Because the money was guaranteed over five years, balances could be carried from one year to the next, so that building went on without the halts that an insufficient annual allowance had caused before.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The financing worked: the ships were built with low cost overruns and few delays. As a deterrent the act failed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> soon matched British building, together beginning 12 battleships, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> answered in 1894 with the Spencer Programme at a cost of over £31 million. The act probably contributed to a naval arms race, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> also enlarged their fleets. It was repealed by the Finance Act 1894, in force from 31 July 1894.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Naval Defence Act 1889” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The “two-power standard”, formally adopted in the <b>Naval Defence Act</b> of 1889, held that the fleet should be at least as strong as the next two largest navies combined.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
